    <div class="by-card by-mt">
      <div class="by-chead"><span class="by-num">01</span><h2>Buy on an exchange</h2></div>
      <p class="by-how">The usual route, and the one that handles the most volume. You open an account, verify your identity, fund it by bank transfer or card, place an order, and then withdraw the coins to your own wallet. Bank transfers are slower to clear than cards and are almost always cheaper. Some exchanges hold your coins by default until you ask for them; a few send every order straight out to an address you supply. The same accounts sell bitcoin back to your bank when that day comes.</p>
      <div class="by-rows">
        <div class="by-row by-cost"><b>What it costs</b><span>A commission, a spread, or both. A commission is a stated percentage of the order. A spread is a markup baked into the quoted price, which is why a service advertising no commission can still be the expensive one. Add the deposit method fee and the on-chain fee to withdraw.</span></div>
        <div class="by-row by-need"><b>What it takes</b><span>Identity verification: legal name, address, date of birth, a photo of a government ID, often a selfie, and sometimes questions about where your money came from. Expect it, and expect it to be permanent.</span></div>
        <div class="by-row by-watch"><b>Watch for</b><span>Leaving the coins on the exchange. An exchange balance is a claim on a company, not bitcoin you hold. <a href="/learn/frozen-deposits">Frozen deposits</a> covers what that has meant in practice.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="by-wlab">Where to go</div>
      
