    <div class="by-card by-mt">
      <div class="by-chead"><span class="by-num">03</span><h2>Use a bitcoin ATM</h2></div>
      <p class="by-how">A machine in a store takes cash and sends bitcoin to an address you scan from your wallet. It is quick, it is physical, and it is available where bank rails are not. It is also the costliest common route by a wide margin; our <a href="/atms">bitcoin ATM guide</a> covers the fees, the limits, and how a purchase actually goes.</p>
      <div class="by-rows">
        <div class="by-row by-cost"><b>What it costs</b><span>Fees run high. Operators charge a service fee and quote a price marked up from the market rate, and the two stack, so the all-in cost is usually several times an exchange's. Compare the machine's quoted rate against the <a href="/price">current price</a> before you feed it a bill.</span></div>
        <div class="by-row by-need"><b>What it takes</b><span>A wallet on your phone to scan, and, above small amounts, a phone number and often an ID scan. Machines are not the anonymous option people assume.</span></div>
        <div class="by-row by-watch"><b>Watch for</b><span>Anyone who told you to use one. Machines are a favorite venue for coached payments, where a caller stays on the line while you deposit cash to an address they supplied. <a href="/learn/scams">Common scams</a> covers the pattern.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="by-wlab">Where to go</div>
      <div class="by-svcs">
